# tools/al/ales_stereo_gate_witness.rish -- stereo_gate: the noise gate carried into stereo, one LINKED DETECTOR, the image the gate refuses to tear (ALES156).
#
# Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus - the creative suite) - waymark ALES - rung ALES156.
# Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-061957_fill-ales156-lotus-stereo-gate.md
# Stands on: lotus/gate.rye (ALES51 -- the mono noise gate, its gate and GateError reused whole for the below-both proof and the fault set), lotus/pan.rye (ALES10 -- the StereoClip and its balance invariant),
# lotus/normalize.rye (ALES103) / lotus/stereo_normalize.rye (ALES129 -- the amplitude-class linked lift this rung answers for the dynamics class), lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2 -- the Clip, sample_max, and the range-law shape).
#
# lotus/stereo_gate.rye opens the whole stereo DYNAMICS family (gate - compressor - limiter - expander) on its simplest member: it gates a span of sample-PAIRS in a master below a magnitude threshold by a ratio, with ONE
# linked detector across the two channels. The detector key per pair is max(|left|, |right|), the louder of the two: at or above the threshold the gate is open and both samples pass byte for byte (the quiet channel
# included -- the image held whole); below it the gate closes and each channel divides its OWN magnitude by the ratio, exactly as ALES51 would to each. Running the mono gate twice would gate one channel while its partner
# plays, tearing the image -- the dynamics-class twin of the bug ALES129 named for amplitude. GateError (BadThreshold, BadRatio, BadRange) reused whole; the linked lift adds no fault. Values only, never a length.
# The crux is the linked-detector law (a pair is gated iff BOTH channels fall below the threshold), the below-both law (a below-both pair equals the mono gate on each byte for byte), the image/sign law (no magnitude
# expands, no sign flips, an out-of-phase quiet pair returns still out of phase), and the knob laws (above the identity, unit ratio the identity everywhere, a higher ratio gating at least as hard, a huge ratio silencing).
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/al/ales_stereo_gate_witness.rish

say "ales-stereo-gate: Language EN — stereo_gate gates a span of sample-pairs in a stereo master below a magnitude threshold by a ratio, with one linked detector across the two channels. For each pair the detector key is the max of the two channels' magnitudes, the louder of the two: at or above the threshold the gate is open and both samples pass byte for byte, the quiet channel included; below it the gate closes and each channel divides its own magnitude by the ratio, sign(x) times m times ratio_den over ratio_num, exactly as the mono gate would to each. It validates threshold, ratio, and span once against the shared length before any write, so a refusal never gates one channel and leaves the other torn. It writes values only, never a length, so the two channels leave balanced; an empty span is a clean no-op on both."
say "ales-stereo-gate: Style Radiant — ALES51 named the mono gate: a sample at or above a magnitude threshold passes byte for byte (the gate is open), a sample below it has its magnitude divided by a ratio and rewritten sign(x) times the quotient (the gate is closed), the compressor's threshold turned upside down, quieting the hiss between words and the room tone in a pause. Carried into stereo, this rung opens the whole dynamics family on the one decision no waveshaper ever faced: linked or independent detection. Running the mono gate twice, each channel deciding on its own magnitude, would gate the quiet right while the loud left plays — the image lurches to one side and chatters, the dynamics-class twin of the bug ALES129 named for amplitude, where independent processing tears the very inter-channel difference that is the stereo image. So this rung links the detector: it decides open or closed once, from both channels together, and applies that one decision to both. The crux is five laws: the linked-detector law (a pair is gated iff both channels fall below the threshold, so a pair loud on one channel and quiet on the other leaves both untouched where mono-gate-twice would quiet the soft one); the below-both law (where the whole pair sits below threshold, each channel equals the mono gate over the same span byte for byte); the image and sign law (an even, sign-preserving attenuation — no magnitude expands, no sign flips, an out-of-phase quiet pair returns still out of phase); the knob laws (above the threshold the identity on both, unit ratio the identity everywhere on both, a higher ratio gating at least as hard and a ratio far past the magnitude silencing a closed pair); and the balance, atomicity, and degenerate law (length held, each refusal leaving both channels untouched and balanced, count zero and the empty span the identity on both)."
say "ales-stereo-gate: Lens TAME — two bounded in-process i16 Clips (left, right) gated by one linked detector, one comparison of the linked key and at most one divide per channel per sample, memoryless (no attack, hold, or release; those are the envelope rungs and their later stereo twins); the two channels enter balanced (the StereoClip invariant asserted on entry) and leave balanced (a gate writes values only, each length asserted equal and within bound on exit); threshold, ratio, and span are validated once against the shared length before either channel is mutated, so a refusal never gates one channel and leaves the other torn; StereoGateError is ALES51's GateError (BadThreshold, BadRatio, BadRange) reused whole, the linked lift adding no fault. Nothing on the audio path can overflow: the ratio is at least one, so a gated magnitude is at most the input's, which already fits i16, and the magnitude is computed in i64 (|sample_min| equals 32768). The linked-detector law is proven against a pair loud on the left and quiet on the right, where stereo_gate holds both channels and the mono gate run on the right alone is shown to genuinely differ (it would gate the quiet channel); the below-both law is proven against the mono gate directly on a both-quiet master, each channel byte for byte; the image and sign law proves no magnitude expands and no sign flips over a mixed master and that an out-of-phase quiet pair returns still exactly out of phase; the knob laws prove above the threshold the identity on both, unit ratio the identity everywhere, a higher ratio monotone, and a huge ratio silencing a closed pair on both channels; and the balance, atomicity, and degenerate cases prove the length held, each of the four refusals leaving both channels untouched and balanced, and an empty span the identity on both; siloed to lotus/, the threshold a magnitude in sample units and the ratio a plain fraction, no real sample rate, no anti-aliasing, no network, no keys, no funds, no device, no speaker."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "lotus/bin/stereo_gate"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p lotus/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build lotus/stereo_gate.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "ales-stereo-gate: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "lotus/bin/stereo_gate.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "ales-stereo-gate: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "ales-stereo-gate: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN ales-stereo-gate' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "ales-stereo-gate: selftest did not print the GREEN ales-stereo-gate line"
